\f1\fs22 Sarah was right; the sickness passed\'97until the same time the next day. She resolutely refused health care for four days, trusting she\'92d recover as a matter of course. On the fifth day, Nancy ignored her denials, and rang for a house-call.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah doubled-up in bed, and waited for the doctor, but no amount of projecting her thoughts forward\'97to a time when she would again be her hearty and hale self\'97dulled her pain. Sarah was simply unpracticed and ill-equipped at ignoring discomfort. It seemed the whole world had shrunk to the boundaries of her skin, nails and hair follicles, and was simultaneously crushing, slicing, dicing, steaming, pan-frying and roasting her to the marrow.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Her hand jerkily grabbed at the bedside remote and stabbed a button. The overhead screens revived with light and, for a moment, she hoped the distraction would dull her nausea; she grimaced as yet another repeat of 
\f3\i Curb
\f1\i0  was playing in which Tony was the guest of honour.
